sand two elections but  you're absolutely right the  institutional checks of the american  government is what brought richard nixon  down the Senate you know holding the  watergate hearings the house holding the  impeachment hearings but at the end of  the day it was the courts I mean it was  the it was a judge sarika  then the Supreme Court ordering that the  tapes be turned over like we would never  know about the tapes if the Senate  hadn't held the Watergate hearings  anyway I just remind you that the  hearings went from may of 73 to 19 to  August of 1973 237 hours and the average  American family watched 30 hours of them  many of you remember watching that it's  just to continue the chicken and egg  game here but with the urban committee  hearings have taken place if it wasn't  for judge sarika in March of 73 ordering  saying I'm not accepting this story that  these guys these burglars are telling me  those hearings so everything did there  were many factors and I think that's  important because out doing the research  for my book how I can't tell you how  many times i stumbledupon reporters  writing about Woodward and Bernstein as  the two reporters who brought down a  president they did not bring down a  president they played a role in Nixon's  demise first of all Nixon was a crook  and Nixon played the biggest role but  without those hearings and actually I  think one of the I won't call him a hero  but one of the linchpins was Alexander  Butterfield Nixon's aide who revealed to  the Senate Watergate hearings that there  were tapes in fact at the time only  about four people knew that there were  tapes and can you tell us what Alexander  Butterfield said to you a few weeks ago  when he came up to you at your book  signing at politics and prose he told me  I was pretty historical moment right now  that one is one last question has to go  full bore on a story like Iraq when the  opposition that is to say the Democratic  Party was so supine at the time I mean  can the press to sort of take off in a  vacuum when there's no political well  that's a very good question and it  certainly helps to have you know the  opposition party out there Fanning the  flames but let's cut back to Watergate  when Woodward and Bernstein were you  know doing the real on the ground shoe  leather reporting in in Watergate I  don't think the Democrats were making a  major issue of this this was during the  72 campaign it wasn't front-page news it  wasn't being a fan the flames I guess  they did file a civil suit early on on  the burglary and that that did play  somewhat of a role but basically they  were out there one in 1972 with all but  one state Massachusetts was the only  state that did not go for Nixon so and  plus the majority of the newspapers at  that time endorsed Nixon so I don't know  if you would call that a climate of fear  but it was certainly not a climate where  other reporters had the support of their  newspapers in fact this was of course  before the internet and so the post was  doing a lot of reporting the new york  times but it wasn't getting out there  the gallup poll in nineteen in november  of 1972 said only forty-eight percent of  the country had ever even heard the word  Watergate well i hope then we can all  endorser the lone wolf version of  reporters being very necessary and that  the Fourth Estate really won't succeed  if we don't have these independent  actors working for the Fourth Estate I  want to remind everyone that we have for  sale out in the lobby both Alicia's book  and Mike's book on hubris and we welcome  the fact that you were here and thank  you for your good questions we  appreciate you being here thanks
 

